INT. MINIATURE GOLF COURSE - CLUBHOUSE - DAY

A cramped clubhouse shaped like a pirate ship. Plastic parrots on every shelf. A faded scoreboard. The air smells of nacho cheese and carpet glue.

DENNIS OKAFOR, 40s, in a sun-bleached polo that reads CAPTAIN HOOK'S, sits behind the counter counting quarters into paper rolls. He is the calmest man alive.

Across the counter, MARISOL BELTRAN, 30s, tidy blazer, clipboard, sharp ponytail, waits with a polite smile.

                    MARISOL
Thank you for seeing me, Mr. Okafor.

                    DENNIS
Call me Dennis. Everybody does.

                    MARISOL
Dennis. I'm with the county inspector's office.

                    DENNIS
That's nice. Quarter?

He offers her one. She declines.

                    MARISOL
This is a routine visit.

                    DENNIS
I love routine.

                    MARISOL
Your last inspection was fourteen months ago.

                    DENNIS
Time flies when you're having fun.

She flips a page. Makes a mark. He keeps counting, perfectly relaxed.

                    MARISOL
I'll need to see the electrical panel.

                    DENNIS
Back by the windmill. Hole nine.

                    MARISOL
And the fire exits.

                    DENNIS
Hole twelve has a very nice one. It's the volcano.

                    MARISOL
The volcano is the fire exit?

                    DENNIS
It's themed.

She almost smiles. Catches herself. Writes something.

                    MARISOL
And your permit for the animal attractions.

                    DENNIS
We don't have animals.

                    MARISOL
The sign out front says LIVE ALLIGATOR.

                    DENNIS
That's a lie. I mean, a joke. A marketing joke.

                    MARISOL
Is it?

                    DENNIS
It's fiberglass. His name is Kevin.

From the back of the clubhouse comes a low, wet THUMP. Then a scrape of claws on linoleum.

Dennis keeps counting. A quarter rolls off the counter.

                    MARISOL
What was that?

                    DENNIS
Pipes.

                    MARISOL
That sounded like a--

                    DENNIS
Old building. Very expressive pipes.

THUMP. Something large and unseen slides into a stack of plastic buckets. They clatter.

Marisol sets down her clipboard. Slowly.

                    MARISOL
Dennis.

                    DENNIS
Yes, ma'am.

                    MARISOL
Open the back room.

Dennis sighs. Finally sets down the quarters. Shuffles to the door, unhooks a small brass latch.

Before he can turn the handle, it bursts open from inside.

BIG LOU PACHECO, 50s, wet suit unzipped to the waist, bandaged forearm, and a cooler under one arm, stumbles out. Behind him, in the dim storeroom, a four-foot alligator blinks at the light.

                    BIG LOU
Dennis, he ate the hush puppies.

Silence. The alligator hisses.

Marisol looks at Dennis. Dennis looks at the ceiling.

                    DENNIS
That's Kevin.

                    MARISOL
That is not fiberglass.

                    DENNIS
He's a different Kevin.
